<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Sahib ibn Abbad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">938–995</span> · Also known as Isma'il ibn Abbad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Sahib ibn Abbad was a Persian statesman and man of letters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> to the Buyid rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muayyad-al-dawla">Mu'ayyad al-Dawla</a> and Fakhr al-Dawla at Ray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> from 976 until his death in 995. A pupil of Ibn al-Amid, he gathered poets and scholars at his court and owned a famous library. He supported the Mu'tazili school of theology and wrote on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabic-language">Arabic language</a>.</p></div>
